"**/query.sql"], ["Read", "/private/tmp/wk/95b2e7e0d0/workbench/query.sql"], ["Bash", "ls -a && git log --oneline | head -20 && grep -rIl -i \"create table\\|create index\" . --exclude-dir=.git | head"], ["Bash", "cat README.md | head -30; echo ---; head -20 commits.txt; echo ---; grep -i -E \"slow|orders|index|migrat\" app.log | head -20"]], "final_message": "The likely cause is `lower(u.email) = '...'` in the `WHERE` clause. Nothing in this directory confirms it, though. There's no schema, no `EXPLAIN` output, and no record of what changed last week. `commits.txt` and `app.log` have nothing relevant except a single \"slow disk\" warning.\n\n**What's wrong with the query**\n\n1. **`lower(u.email)` can't use a normal index on `users.email`.** Wrapping the column in a function makes the planner scan every row in `users` and lowercase each one. That's fine at 10k users and painful at several million. A slowdown that appeared \"last week\" often means the table crossed a size threshold, or an index was dropped or rebuilt, or the planner's statistics changed.\n2. **`SELECT *` over a join** pulls every column from both tables. That includes any wide columns such as JSON blobs or text notes, and it prevents index-only scans. It also gives you duplicate column names (`id`, `created_at`, and so on), which some ORMs handle badly.\n3. **`orders.user_id` may have no index.** After the lookup finds the user, the join needs an index on `orders(user_id)`. Without one, it scans all of `orders`.\n\n**Fixes, in order of likely payoff**\n\nPostgres syntax; adjust for your database:\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Expression index that matches the query\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n\n-- 2. Make sure the join side is indexed\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n```\n\nThen select only the columns the page needs:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.status, o.total\nFROM orders o\nJOIN users u ON u.id = o.user_id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\nAlternatives to the expression index:\n- If emails are already normalized on write, drop the `lower()` and use a plain unique index on `email`.\n- On Postgres, a `citext` column does case-insensitive matching with a normal index.\n- On MySQL with a case-insensitive collation, `lower()` is unnecessary.\n\n**To confirm before changing anything**\n\nRun `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on the query. A `Seq Scan on users` or `Seq Scan on orders` confirms the diagnosis. Also check:\n- Whether an index on `users.email` or `orders.user_id` was dropped or rebuilt last week.\n- Whether the table sizes jumped, for example after a bulk import.\n- Whether the ORM or app code changed in a way that now sends `lower(...)`. If the old query was `WHERE u.email = ...`, that would explain why it only got slow now.\n- Whether the page runs this query many times (N+1). Eight seconds from one indexed lookup would be surprising.\n\nIf you paste the `EXPLAIN` output and the table definitions, I can say which of these it is.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}